\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Users should be able to mark a container (aggregation) as "closed" so nothing more can be added, and reopen it if needed. You can't close a parent container until every container inside it is already closed. And the moment a container is closed, it gets deleted.
\f1\i0 \
Now the M-Files assessment:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Open / close an active aggregation
\f1\b0  \'97 
\f2\b Yes, with configuration.
\f1\b0  You model the container as an object type (Project, Case, File, etc.) and use 
\f2\b workflow states
\f1\b0  \'97 an "Active/Open" state and a "Closed" state. Users move it between them. Standard M-Files capability.\

\f2\b Can't close a parent until all children are closed
\f1\b0  \'97 
\f2\b Yes, but it needs custom logic.
\f1\b0  This isn't out-of-the-box. You enforce it with a 
\f2\b workflow state condition or a compliance/validation script
\f1\b0  that checks every child object's state before allowing the transition to "Closed." Doable and reliable, but it's a configured control, not a checkbox.\

\f2\b Closed aggregations are immediately destroyed
\f1\b0  \'97 
\f3 \uc0\u9888 \u65039 
\f1  
\f2\b Technically yes, but stop and check this one.
\f1\b0  M-Files 
\f0\i can
\f1\i0  trigger deletion on entering a state (via workflow action/script), so the capability exists. But the requirement as written is almost certainly wrong or garbled. In records management, 
\f0\i closing
\f1\i0  and 
\f0\i destroying
\f1\i0  are completely separate lifecycle events \'97 you close a container, it sits under a retention period, and it's destroyed later per a disposal schedule. "Close = instant destruction" would mean records are wiped the second they're finalised, which defeats the entire point of a records system and would likely breach retention obligations.\
My strong suggestion: flag that third clause back to whoever wrote the sheet. It reads like two separate MoReq2010 requirements (closing aggregations, and destroying aggregations that have reached end-of-retention) got merged into one sentence. Answering a flat "yes" against it risks committing to behaviour nobody actually wants.\
So a fair way to record it: first two parts 
\f2\b Yes (with configuration)
\f1\b0 ; third part 
\f2\b needs clarification \'97 likely a wording error
\f1\b0 .\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
